      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am not sure this test does what it is meant to. reg_size is BIT(ESR_ELx_SAS) from the faulting instruction, so the host picks it from 1, 2, 4 and 8, and the test asks whether access_size is a multiple of reg_size rather than whether they match.\n\nFor an 8-byte access to a 4-byte register, 4 \u0026 7 is non-zero, so the handler is skipped and the loop falls through to pkvm_handle_forward_req and the access reaches the hardware unmediated. For GICR_CTLR that is the one register this series exists to mediate. Going the other way, 1- and 2-byte accesses pass the test for every register, because access_size \u0026 0 and access_size \u0026 1 are both 0, so a single byte write is handled as a full width write of that value: a 1-byte write of 0 at offset 0 takes the handle_lpi_disable path. There is no alignment check on offset either, so a 4-byte write at GICR_PROPBASER + 4 is handled as a write of that value to the whole 64-bit register.\n\nCould the test be reg_size \u003d\u003d access_size with offset aligned to access_size, and deny rather than forward when the offset is covered by a handler but the access does not match? The same table walk serves its_handlers, and in the GITS_BASER handler the mismatch turns into an unaligned 64-bit access to Device memory from EL2, which I have commented on separately there.",
      "revId": "8d9ca225dc214be92a6887c0e63a59cddc47e42f",
